WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:11.673
A dashboard that took two seconds now loads in twenty milliseconds after one change: a cache. But stale data and eviction rules are where interviews dig in.

00:00:12.873 --> 00:00:13.802
Pause and think.

00:00:14.202 --> 00:00:17.499
Which data structures give O of 1 for an LRU cache?

00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:25.214
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:31.984
This lesson is about caches: keeping hot data close, and deciding what to throw away.

00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:43.213
New word: cache. A small fast store of recently used data so you don't fetch it from the slow place again. Picture it like this: Keeping the salt next to the stove instead of in the basement.

00:00:45.717 --> 00:00:48.632
Imagine a small desk next to a huge library.

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:55.467
Books you are using right now sit on the desk, so grabbing them is instant. Fetching from the library takes minutes.

00:00:55.917 --> 00:01:00.782
The desk only holds five books. When a sixth arrives, one has to go back.

00:01:01.232 --> 00:01:04.436
The eviction policy is the rule for which book goes back.

00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:12.706
New word: eviction. Removing an item from a cache to make room. Picture it like this: Clearing a shelf to fit new stock.

00:01:15.256 --> 00:01:19.784
LRU means least recently used. Throw out the book you have not touched for the longest.

00:01:19.823 --> 00:01:32.582
New word: L R U. Least Recently Used: a cache rule that throws out whatever was used longest ago. Picture it like this: Clearing the fridge by tossing what nobody has touched in the longest time.

00:01:34.232 --> 00:01:38.748
Keep a doubly linked list in use order, plus a hash map from key to node.

00:01:38.865 --> 00:01:47.480
New word: linked list. A chain of nodes, each pointing to the next. Picture it like this: A treasure hunt where each clue points to the next.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:57.527
New word: node. One point in a graph or tree; also one machine in a cluster. Picture it like this: A city on a map, or one computer in a group.

00:01:59.177 --> 00:02:04.471
On every access, unlink the node and move it to the front. Both steps are constant time.

00:02:04.921 --> 00:02:06.930
When full, evict from the back.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:13.458
LFU means least frequently used. Throw out the book you used the fewest times.

00:02:13.477 --> 00:02:23.926
New word: L F U. Least Frequently Used: a cache rule that evicts what's used least often. Picture it like this: Giving away clothes you wear the least.

00:02:25.527 --> 00:02:30.542
The constant time version keeps one list per use count, plus the current minimum count.

00:02:30.992 --> 00:02:35.044
A get moves a key from the count k list to the count k plus one list.

00:02:35.494 --> 00:02:41.160
LFU protects popular items from a one time scan, which would flush an LRU cache.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:43.938
More words you will hear.

00:02:44.388 --> 00:02:47.743
Hit rate is the fraction of lookups served from the cache.

00:02:48.193 --> 00:02:55.031
TTL, time to live, expires an entry after a fixed time, so stale data cannot live forever.

00:02:55.077 --> 00:03:04.423
New word: T T L. Time To Live: how long a cached value or packet is allowed to exist. Picture it like this: A "best before" date on food.

00:03:06.027 --> 00:03:15.083
Write through updates the cache and database together. Write back updates the cache first and flushes later: faster, but you can lose data in a crash.

00:03:15.533 --> 00:03:22.453
Cache aside means the application checks the cache, and on a miss, loads from the database and fills the cache.

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.219
Systems examples.

00:03:25.669 --> 00:03:31.590
CPU caches use pseudo LRU in hardware because true LRU is too expensive per set.

00:03:31.743 --> 00:03:44.967
New word: C P U. Central Processing Unit: the main general-purpose processor that runs programs step by step. Picture it like this: The head chef who can cook anything, one dish at a time.

00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:52.259
A CI build cache keys outputs by a hash of the inputs, so a hit is always correct.

00:03:52.393 --> 00:04:04.514
New word: C I. Continuous Integration: every code change is automatically built and tested. Picture it like this: A quality inspector who checks every part as soon as it comes off the line.

00:04:06.143 --> 00:04:14.955
And watch for the stampede: when a hot key expires, thousands of requests miss at once. Fix it with a lock per key or early random refresh.

00:04:16.305 --> 00:04:21.959
A cache is a fast desk with an eviction rule. Practice LRU Cache, then LFU Cache.

00:04:21.949 --> 00:04:23.203
Back to the question.

00:04:23.603 --> 00:04:26.900
Which data structures give O of 1 for an LRU cache?

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.610
A hash map plus a doubly linked list.

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:31.681
Quick recap.

00:04:32.081 --> 00:04:33.637
Keep hot data close.

00:04:34.037 --> 00:04:35.686
Eviction picks what to drop.

00:04:36.086 --> 00:04:38.222
Invalidation keeps data fresh.
